UID:nYUWt9qi0sjo2LfC4yPBE
SUMMARY:Art + Climate CONVERSATIONS: Emily Johnson
DTSTAMP:20260716T055638Z
DTSTART:20260729T183000Z
DESCRIPTION:Presented by DW Art + Climate Partners6:30 pm\, Wednesday\, Jul
	y 29\, 2026Suggested Ticket Price: $25 (pay-what-you-wish) Get Tickets10% 
	of ticket sales will be donated to the Texas Tribal Buffalo Project\nThe D
	iverseWorks Art + Climate Partners present Emily Johnson as the inaugural 
	speaker for Art + Climate CONVERSATIONS. Join Houstonians\, climate advoca
	tes\, dance and art enthusiasts\, and artists alike on the evening of July
	 29th for a talk by Johnson\, a Yup’ik multi-disciplinary artist\, choreog
	rapher\, land and water protector\, and organizer for justice\, sovereignt
	y and well-being. In October 2026\, Johnson’s Overflow Radio will be prese
	nted at DiverseWorks. \nArt + Climate CONVERSATIONS highlights artists and
	 creatives that are leading the way on addressing climate-related issues t
	hrough their work. The DW Art + Climate Partners support and advise on cli
	mate programming and future projects. DiverseWorks’ Art + Climate program 
	began in 2023-24 and consists of a climate action plan that guides the org
	anization\; programming that increases awareness of the effects of climate
	 change\; support for artists to develop environmentally sustainable pract
	ices\; and the development of a new\, climate-forward facility for artists
	\, community members\, and other non-profit organizations to share. \nSpea
	ker BioEmily Johnson is a Bessie Award-winning choreographer\, Guggenheim\
	, Native Arts and Cultures Foundation\, and United States Artists Fellow\,
	 and recipient of the Doris Duke Artist Award. She is based in Lenapehokin
	g / New York City. Emily is of the Yup’ik Nation\, and since 1998 has crea
	ted work that considers the experience of sensing and seeing performance. 
	Her dances function as portals and care processions\, they engage audience
	ship within and through space\, time\, and environment — interacting with 
	a place’s architecture\, peoples\, history and role in building futures. E
	mily is trying to make a world where performance is part of life\; where p
	erformance is an integral part of our connection to each other\, our envir
	onment\, our stories\, our past\, present and future. \nHer choreography a
	nd gatherings have been presented across what is currently called the Unit
	ed States and Australia. Her large-scale project\, Then a Cunning Voice an
	d A Night We Spend Gazing at Stars is an all-night outdoor performance gat
	hering taking place amongst 84 community-hand-made quilts. It premiered in
	 Lenapehoking (NYC) in 2017\, and was presented in Zhigaagoong (Chicago) i
	n 2019. She choreographed the Santa Fe Opera production of Doctor Atomic\,
	 directed by Peter Sellars in 2018. Being Future Being\, premiered on Tong
	va Land in Los Angeles in 2022. In 2011\, DiverseWorks presented her perfo
	rmance The Thank-You Bar in conjunction with the exhibition This is Displa
	cement: Native Artists Consider the Relationship Between Land and Identity
	 (co-curated by Johnson and Diné visual artist Carolyn Lee Anderson).  \nE
	mily’s writing has been published and commissioned by The Open Society Uni
	versity Network’s Center for Human Rights and the Arts\, ArtsLink Australi
	a\, unMagazine\, Dance Research Journal (University of Cambridge Press)\; 
	SFMOMA\; Transmotion Journal\, University of Kent\; Movement Research Jour
	nal\; Pew Center for Arts and Heritage\; and the compilation Imagined Thea
	ters: Writing for a Theoretical Stage (Routledge). \nEmily hosts monthly c
	eremonial fires on Mannahatta in partnership with Abrons Arts Center and K
	ai Recollet. She was the Pueblo Opera Cultural Council Diplomat at Santa F
	e Opera 2018-2020\, and a lead organizer of First Nations Dialogues. She w
	as a co-compiler of the documents\, Creating New Futures: Guidelines for E
	thics and Equity in the Performing Arts and Notes for Equitable Funding\, 
	was a member of Creative Time’s inaugural Think Tank\, and serves as a wor
	king consortium member for First Nations Performing Arts.\n\nhttps://diver
	seworks.org/event/art-climate-conversations-emily-johnson/\n\nFrom Diverse
	Works
URL:https://diverseworks.org/event/art-climate-conversations-emily-johnson/
LOCATION:DiverseWorks – MATCHBOX 2\, 3400 Main Street\, Houston\, TX\, 7700
	2\, United States
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:diverseworks
CATEGORIES:DiverseWorks
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8Rjo9QIxksbcMJdh3vDWF
SUMMARY:Ionospheric O⁺ Transport to the Inner Magnetosphere: A Global MHD a
	nd Test-Particle Study
DTSTAMP:20260716T055638Z
DTSTART:20260729T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Yiwen Zhu  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defense
	  Department: Physics &amp\; Astronomy  Location: Herman Brown Hall HBH 25
	4  During geomagnetic storms\, ionospheric oxygen (O⁺) can become a signif
	icant constituent of Earth's inner magnetosphere\, whose storm-time partic
	le buildup drives the main-phase depression of the surface magnetic field.
	 The ionosphere supplies this oxygen from two main regions: the dayside po
	lar cusp and the nightside auroral oval. Their relative contribution to th
	e inner magnetosphere\, and how each population is energized as it moves i
	nward\, remain poorly quantified\, in part because the transport spans sca
	les that no single model resolves.  In this thesis\, we follow ionospheric
	 oxygen from both sources to the inner magnetosphere using a global magnet
	ohydrodynamic simulation (GAMERA/MAGE) coupled one-way to a test-particle 
	tracer (CHIMP). Because MAGE couples models spanning a range of scales and
	 regions\, it resolves both the mesoscale bursty bulk flows (BBFs) of the 
	plasma sheet that drive earthward transport and the particle dynamics of t
	he inner magnetosphere. For a real storm\, we compare how cusp and nightsi
	de-auroral oxygen reach the plasma sheet and the inner magnetosphere. With
	in an adiabatic (guiding-center) framework\, we decompose the energy gain 
	along the transport into its Fermi\, betatron\, and E×B contributions and 
	quantify each\, and we characterize where the oxygen departs from adiabati
	c behavior. The two sources follow different paths: cusp oxygen takes the 
	long route through the lobe\, while auroral oxygen enters the closed plasm
	a sheet more directly. We also identify a drop-down channel\, in which mes
	oscale plasma-sheet flows pull cusp oxygen down to the equatorial plasma s
	heet and let it enter the inner magnetosphere more efficiently.  To place 
	the contribution on a quantitative footing\, we weight the test particles 
	by O⁺ outflow fluences computed directly from the simulation's MHD variabl
	es\, include charge-exchange loss\, and compare the modeled oxygen against
	 Van Allen Probes observations. Cusp oxygen enters the inner magnetosphere
	 at higher energy and auroral oxygen at lower energy\, so their relative c
	ontribution depends on the number density of ions each source delivers. Th
	rough two different storms\, we show two cases in which the delivered numb
	ers differ\, giving different relative and absolute contributions of the t
	wo sources to the inner magnetosphere.  Finally\, we return to the cusp it
	self. Predicting where the cusp lies from the upstream solar wind is opera
	tionally useful. We develop a machine-learning model\, gradient-boosted de
	cision trees trained on DMSP spacecraft cusp crossings\, that predicts the
	 cusp location from solar-wind and interplanetary-magnetic-field condition
	s\, offering a fast alternative to a full simulation.  Together\, these ch
	apters connect the ionospheric sources of inner-magnetosphere oxygen to it
	s delivery and energization: how cusp and auroral O⁺ reach the inner magne
	tosphere\, how each is energized along the way\, and how much each contrib
	utes across storms. The cusp machine-learning model complements this with 
	a fast predictor of the cusp location from the upstream solar wind.  Join 
	via Zoom: https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/97738620987?pwd=OmGDYN5xMiERaeL
	kGnHvTsx6K0IIOF.1 (https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/97738620987?pwd=OmGDYN
	5xMiERaeLkGnHvTsx6K0IIOF.1)  (Department : Physics &amp\; Astronomy)\n\nht
	tps://events.rice.edu/event/433274-ionospheric-o-transport-to-the-inner-ma
	gnetosphere-a-\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/433274-ionospheric-o-transport-to-the-inn
	er-magnetosphere-a-
